From 4c8e094ee8879a7bed7860718694c12a8894da50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 21:31:29 -0700
Subject: [RFC net-next v1 1/1] net: flow_dissector: adaptive auto-enable
 (packet-window controller)

Seven opt-in gates are seven policy decisions the operator has to get
right. This RFC lets the kernel manage the byte-identical gates itself
from the per-shape counters, so "turn on the fast path" becomes one
knob instead of seven:

  net.flow_dissector.auto                 0/1 (static key; manual
                                          knobs apply when 0)
  net.flow_dissector.auto_window_packets  decision window
                                          (default 1,000,000)
  net.flow_dissector.auto_exclude         bitmask of shapes auto must
                                          leave alone (default: mpls),
                                          so single gates can be pinned
                                          by hand while auto runs

Decisions are driven by a packet-count window, not a wall-clock tick:
every ~auto_window_packets dissects the worker re-evaluates each
byte-identical shape's windowed eligible fraction (occurrences +
fast_hits over the window) against its measured break-even
p_be = C / (S + C) and flips the gate. This is traffic-proportional: a
busy link adapts in milliseconds, an idle link does not churn, and
adaptation speed is one intuitive knob (the same shape as lib/dim
interrupt moderation).

Design points:

  - Decide on window *deltas*, never cumulative counters -- a
    since-boot ratio asymptotically freezes and stops adapting. The
    worker snapshots the counters each decision and diffs.
  - Sample on the hot path, flip in process context. With auto off the
    packet path pays one not-taken static branch (fd_auto_on_key --
    the same discipline as the gates themselves); with auto on it adds
    a per-cpu increment and a compare against a cached threshold (no
    division on the packet path; the window/ncpus threshold is
    recomputed on sysctl writes and per worker run, which also tracks
    CPU hotplug lazily) that schedule_work()s, with preemption
    disabled across the counter update so a migration cannot reset a
    stranger's window. The summation and the static_branch flips
    (text_poke, which can sleep) run in the worker, which the sysctl
    handlers cancel_work_sync() against before touching shared
    windowing state. Counter deltas are clamped (64-bit per-cpu reads
    can tear on 32-bit; a torn read must not drive a flip).
  - The per-shape policy is a pure function (fd_auto_decide()):
    hysteresis (enable above break-even + 10pp, disable below
    break-even - 5pp), a dwell of 3 consecutive windows, and dwell
    saturation so a rate-capped decision stays pending instead of
    wrapping. Being pure, it is unit-tested directly: the KUnit case
    covers the dwell requirement, both margin boundaries, dwell reset
    on a dip, the deadband holding both states, the low-break-even
    clamp, and saturation.

A >= 1s per-shape flip-rate cap keeps a crafted burst from forcing
text_poke storms while letting a traffic shift that touches several
shapes adapt in one window. Shape management is a whitelist -- a shape
the controller does not explicitly know is never managed, so the
UDP-tunnel descent gates (separate series) can never be auto-managed
even if they later join the counter enum; enabling them changes
hashing and must stay deliberate. mpls is excluded by default via
auto_exclude (its saving barely exceeds its miss cost on in-order
cores). The vlan/qinq coupling is respected, including under pins:
while vlan is excluded, qinq is left unmanaged too. An attached netns
BPF flow dissector zeroes the controller's input (those dissects are
deliberately not counted), so it will disable the gates -- consistent,
since the gates are unreachable behind the program, and documented.
The gates are global static keys patching shared text, so the
controller is host-wide by construction; the per-netns escape hatch
remains the netns BPF flow dissector.

Applies on top of "net: flow_dissector: opt-in byte-identical fast
paths for common shapes".

